Procedure
Step 1: Verify Docker

Run:

docker --version
docker ps

Docker should be running and accessible.

Step 2: Check Kubernetes

Run:

minikube status
kubectl config current-context

Your earlier error indicates that Kubernetes is not reachable, so we need to fix this before proceeding.

Step 3: Start Kubernetes using Minikube

If Minikube is installed but stopped, run:

minikube start --driver=docker

Then verify:

kubectl get nodes
kubectl cluster-info

You should see a Kubernetes node in the Ready state.

Step 4: Deploy a Docker image in Kubernetes

Use the existing Python image from your previous Docker experiment:

kubectl create deployment python-demo --image=python:3.10

Check the deployment and pod:

kubectl get deployments
kubectl get pods

The pod may take a little time to download the image and start.

Step 5: Verify the integration
kubectl describe deployment python-demo
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

Replace <pod-name> with the actual pod name shown by kubectl get pods.

Kubernetes will manage the application through its deployment and pod resources, while Docker provides the container runtime through Minikube's Docker-based setup.

Step 6: Clean up after the experiment

When you've completed your demonstration:

kubectl delete deployment python-demo

This removes the test deployment without deleting your Docker installation.
